Building jlib on Linux via Docker
=================================

The source is copied into the image and compiled during the image build, not
bind-mounted.  On macOS a bind mount crosses the VM's filesystem
virtualization layer, which makes C++ compilation dramatically slower.

    # build the toolchain image and compile jlib inside it
    docker build -t jlib-build .

    # drop into a shell on the built tree
    docker run --rm -ti jlib-build

Inside the container the build lives at /src/jlib/build:

    cd /src/jlib/build
    make check
    make -j"$(nproc)"

configure prints a feature summary saying which optional modules were enabled
and which were skipped for want of a dependency.  On Linux it should report
x11, glx and the joystick API as present; on macOS those are absent, GLFW is
the portable windowing backend, and Metal is the GPU compute backend -- so the
container never builds jlib/metal and every test that needs a GPU skips there.

Dependencies install in their own layer, so editing source re-runs only the
autogen/configure/make steps.  A .dockerignore keeps the host's build trees
and generated autotools output out of the image so the container always
bootstraps for its own platform.

The image also installs dovecot, nginx and tinyproxy, for the *_live_test
programs: net_imap_live_test, net_pop3_live_test, net_http_live_test and
sys_proxy_live_test.  Each starts a real server on a high port, drives jlib
against it, and stops it again.

They exist because a std::istringstream produces only the responses somebody
thought of, and so does an in-process server you wrote yourself: an IMAP
literal, a Date and an ETag in an order nobody chose, a proxy's 403, are all
things only a server decides to send.  The in-process servers -- tests/
httpserver.hh, and the scripted one in net_imap_sasl_test -- are for the
opposite case, a chunked body whose data looks like its own framing, which no
real server will produce on request.  Both kinds are needed.

Outside the container those daemons are usually absent, and the tests report
SKIP (exit 77) rather than failing.  It needs to run as root: Dovecot refuses to
start imap-login as root, so the config leaves that process on the package's
own unprivileged user, and the seeded Maildir has to be chowned to an ordinary
uid because Dovecot also refuses a mail uid of 0.

No model file is copied in -- .dockerignore keeps *.gguf out, since they are
gigabytes -- so the ai_* tests run whatever half needs no model and skip the
rest.  ai_engine_test exercises its bookkeeping, ai_tokenizer_test its
pre-tokenizer, and neither loads weights.  The GPU kernels are verified on
macOS only; what the container establishes for them is that they compile under
a second compiler and that the host paths still agree.

Note: the crypt_groth_test allocates heavily.  If it is killed with exit 137
the container hit its memory ceiling -- raise the Docker VM's RAM allocation.
